by nicolas-grekas at 2026-09-02T06:55:23Z

Thank you @WedgeSama for this work and for your patience. I reviewed the PR in depth and pushed a rework in place, building on the discussion above. The description is updated to match. Here is what changed and why, so everyone can react.

**Naming.** `AsFormType` is renamed to `AsFormData` and `Type` to `FormField`. This addresses @stof's point that `AsFormType` reads like tagging a `form.type` service, and the `Field` vs `Type` debate: the class is the form's data, which is the `data_class` vocabulary every Form user already knows, and `FormField` stays self-explaining when seen alone on a property. Better names welcome, but I think this direction is right.

**Everything moved to compile time**, as @GromNaN suggested. `FormPass` now reads the attributes itself, using `findTaggedResourceIds('form.data_class')`, and compiles one definition per class made of plain literals. No runtime reflection is left, invalid declarations fail the container build instead of the first request, and editing the class rebuilds the container in debug mode. Option values are restricted to scalars, arrays and enums; anything else is rejected at compile time with a message pointing to form type classes. Note that inline constraints in options are not needed anyway: on a data class, constraints belong on the properties as regular `#[Assert\...]` attributes. Compile-time reading is also what would later allow resolving `Autowire`-style option values into service references, which the runtime approach could never do.

**The `framework.form.use_attribute` option is gone**, as @GromNaN and @Jean-Beru asked. Adding the attribute to an autoconfigured class is the opt-in, exactly like `#[AsMessage]`. No new configuration, no new services: the pass does everything.

**The public surface shrank to the two attributes and the resource tag.** The `Metadata\` namespace, the loader abstraction, `MetadataException` and `MetadataExtension` are removed; the derived type and its identity interface are internal. The reasoning: promoting an internal class to public API later is free, while removing a shipped interface is impossible, and each of those abstractions had exactly one implementation with no second one planned. A standalone entry point for component-only users can be added the day someone asks for it, without touching anything shipped here.

**Fixed along the way:**
- An abstract class with the attribute broke container compilation ("must not be abstract"). Abstract data classes now work and can serve as parent types; there is an end-to-end test for that.
- `debug:form App\Dto\UserDto` failed with "Could not find type" while shell completion suggests exactly that FQCN. It now works.
- The debug command wiring used `setArgument(6, ...)`, which broke any container defining the command with fewer arguments (the reason the existing `FormPassTest` had to be padded). It now uses a named argument and the original test is restored unchanged.
- Renaming a field while also setting `property_path` silently discarded the user's `property_path`. It is now a compile error.
- Added the missing CHANGELOG entries and integration tests that go from a compiled container to a submitted, mapped form.

The low-deps concern about `findTaggedResourceIds` no longer applies: the method exists since DI 7.3 and the component now requires `^7.4|^8.0` for dev.

Behavior of the examples in the description is covered by tests. Reviews welcome, in particular on the attribute names and on the compile-time-only stance for option values.

